 near the center','Create the ultimate contemporary Japanese tattoo composition as one interconnected world across the body. A dragon moves from storm cloud into turbulent water while a tiger travels through rock and wind below and a distant human figure provides scale. Peonies and seasonal vegetation appear only where compositionally and culturally appropriate. Every wave cloud leaf whisker piece of fabric and body movement responds to the same environmental forces. Use changing scale dramatic cropping foreground overlap and anatomy so no single viewpoint reveals the entire artwork. The result should feel deeply grounded in Japanese tattoo composition while demonstrating how far that language can be taken as contemporary fine art')
$b['A dragon head turns sharply back toward its own body as though something behind it has caught its attention. The neck makes one compact coil beneath the head while two cloud forms create depth without overwhelming the piece'] = 'The tattoo: A dragon head turns sharply back toward its own body as though something behind it has caught its attention. The neck makes one compact coil beneath the head while two cloud forms create depth without overwhelming the piece. Powerful, elegant, supernatural. Create exceptional Japanese tattoo artwork deeply grounded in the compositional principles and visual language of Japanese tattooing while allowing ambitious contemporary artistic interpretation. Build each design around a powerful dominant subject, clear directional movement, confident line structure, substantial black, controlled saturated color, intentional untouched skin and an intelligent relationship between foreground, middle ground and background. Use established Japanese tattoo subjects such as dragons, koi, tigers, snakes, cranes, hawks, phoenixes, masks, warriors, human figures, peonies, chrysanthemums, cherry blossoms, maple leaves, pine, bamboo, waves, rocks, wind and clouds with visual and cultural respect. When using specific mythological, religious, folkloric or symbolic figures, preserve their recognizable identity and do not invent false cultural meanings. Background elements are not decorative filler: water must have coherent current and force, wind must influence leaves, fabric, hair and atmosphere, clouds must create depth and supernatural space, rocks must ground the composition and vegetation should contribute season, direction and balance. Do not automatically include every Japanese background element in every tattoo. Match compositional complexity to physical scale. Small tattoos should contain one exceptional idea with minimal support. Medium tattoos should generally use one dominant subject and one meaningful environmental relationship. Large tat