ubjects such as dragons, koi, tigers, snakes, cranes, hawks, phoenixes, masks, warriors, human figures, peonies, chrysanthemums, cherry blossoms, maple leaves, pine, bamboo, waves, rocks, wind and clouds with visual and cultural respect. When using specific mythological, religious, folkloric or symbolic figures, preserve their recognizable identity and do not invent false cultural meanings. Background elements are not decorative filler: water must have coherent current and force, wind must influence leaves, fabric, hair and atmosphere, clouds must create depth and supernatural space, rocks must ground the composition and vegetation should contribute season, direction and balance. Do not automatically include every Japanese background element in every tattoo. Match compositional complexity to physical scale. Small tattoos should contain one exceptional idea with minimal support. Medium tattoos should generally use one dominant subject and one meaningful environmental relationship. Large tattoos can develop subject, support and environment. Monumental tattoos can become complete interconnected worlds. Create a deliberate mixture of small, medium, large and monumental tattoos rather than defaulting to sleeves, full legs and back pieces. Explore unusual viewpoints, dramatic cropping, foreshortening, partial concealment, subjects entering or leaving the frame, asymmetrical movement and compositions that reveal themselves as the wearer turns. Design specifically for the stated male or female body placement so curves, joints, shoulders, limbs, hips, ribs, chest and back actively contribute to the artwork. Do not merely paste a Japanese subject onto generic waves. Do not create repetitive dragon plus flower plus cloud formulas. Do not turn every tiger into a roaring frontal portrait, every koi into the same side-view fish or every Hannya into the same centered mask. Do not fill empty areas automatically. Preserve breathing room and intentional transitions into bare skin. Avoid Western photorealism, generic fantasy concept art, anime styling unless specifically requested, fake kanji, invented Japanese symbols, random cultural mashups and meaningless decorative motifs. The goal is not to imitate one individual artist. The goal is to understand the extraordinary compositional intelligence of master Japanese tattooing and use that language to create original, beautiful, powerful and tattooable artwork ranging from a single forearm piece to a complete body-scale environment; no watermark. In the right panel it is on a man, full back extending selectively onto shoulders'
